{
 "reference_line": "Independent closed-form complex-impedance implementation of selected IEC 60909-0:2016 kernels, the EasyPower/Parise legacy CCM equation set, and three Schneider Electric published cross-checks, written from the standard and the published documents by bench-src/gen_protectioncoordination.py; no Protection Coordination Studio code is imported and the generator never reads the artifact or the page.",
 "citations": [
  "IEC 60909-0:2016, 4.2 eq. (29) - I_k'' = c U_n/(sqrt(3)|Z_k|)",
  "IEC 60909-0:2016, 4.2 eq. (31)/(34) - I_k2'' = c U_n/|Z_1+Z_2| = (sqrt(3)/2) I_k3'' when Z_2 = Z_1",
  "IEC 60909-0:2016, 4.2 eq. (52) - I_k1'' = sqrt(3) c U_n/|Z_1+Z_2+Z_0|",
  "IEC 60909-0:2016, 4.3.1.2 eq. (55)-(56) and method C - i_p = kappa sqrt(2) I_k'', kappa = 1.02 + 0.98 exp(-3 R/X), R/X = (R_c/X_c)(f_c/f) at f_c = 0.4 f",
  "IEC 60909-0:2016, 6.1 eq. (5)-(6) - network feeder impedance and its referral",
  "IEC 60909-0:2016, 6.2 eq. (7)-(9) - two-winding transformer Z_T, R_T, X_T",
  "IEC 60909-0:2016, 6.6 eq. (18) - Z_G = x_d'' U_rG^2/S_rG; the K_G correction is NOT applied, see reference_limits",
  "EasyPower 11.1 Help, TCC Options, Characteristic Current Method - i_dc(0.5 cycle) and equivalent X/R equations; cites G. Parise, IEEE Trans. Industry Applications 31(1), 1995",
  "Schneider Electric 0100DB2301 Electrical Distribution Fundamentals Design Guide, System Protection - delta-wye secondary L-N fault gives 57.7% of maximum three-phase primary fault current",
  "Schneider Electric 0100DB2301, System Protection, Add a Generator / Figure 55 - bus 02 19.318 kA before and 24.672 kA after adding the 15 MW generator (926.2 A rated, x_d'' = 19%)",
  "Schneider Electric FA405803 - 400 kVA / 410 V / u_kr 4% transformer, I_k'' = I_rT/u_kr on a stiff primary"
 ],
 "tolerance_line": "The tolerances of the 2026-08-12 artifact are kept unchanged so that a verdict can be compared with it: current KPIs +-0.005 kA, kappa +-0.0005, CCM X/R +-0.005, the L-L/3-phase ratio +-0.0008 absolute, the 57.7% transformer ratio +-0.002 absolute, and the Schneider generator increment 0.5% relative because the published diagram values and generator data are rounded. No tolerance was widened; the observed deviation is recorded next to every graded value.",
 "reference_limits": [
  "IEC 60909-0:2016 6.1 eq. (6) writes Z_Q = c U_nQ^2/S_kQ\u2033, so eq. (29) at the feeder terminals returns the quoted I_kQ\u2033. The tool uses Z_Q = U_nQ^2/S_kQ\u2033 and applies c only in the equivalent voltage source, which makes its terminal current c x the quoted value: 13 777.68 A against 13 121.60 A for the 250 MVA / 11 kV feeder of analytic_source_3ph. The tool's own regression test asserts the factor c, so this is a declared convention and not a slip. The cases here take Z_Q = U_nQ^2/S_kQ\u2033 as a stated case input and grade only eq. (29)/(31)/(34)/(52)/(55)/(56) on it; the mapping (U_n, S_kQ\u2033) -> Z_Q is NOT established by this benchmark. The four cases that run at c = 1 are unaffected, because there the two conventions coincide.",
  "The generator impedance correction K_G of IEC 60909-0:2016 6.6 eq. (18) is not applied by the tool and cannot be computed from its generator inputs, which carry no rated power factor. The generator branch reference is Z_G = x_d\u2033 U^2/S_rG as entered, so K_G is NOT established.",
  "The six networks are the fixtures of the tool's own regression tests. A fixture is an input, not a reference: every reference value above is computed in this file from the cited clause, and none of them is read back from the tool."
 ],
